 been screaming about to anybody who  will listen since the day Donald Trump  was elected elections are not shaped by  people who change their minds but they  are changed rather by who decides to  vote I've been  saying four years now trying to convince  Trumpets to vote for a Democrat is not a  strategy and it is a waste of time  finding people on our side and getting  them to vote is the way to go there's  this conventional wisdom which is not  always so wise that there's something  like if you have 55% turnout in the US  which is a common turnout for elections  that 15% of that 55% that chooses to  vote are so-called swing voters and they  turn the election you have to convince  those swing voters to vote for your  candidate and this assumes way more  rationality effort and consideration  than most voters are actually putting  into voting decisions it's not a  pejorative ad hominem it's just the  reality and to quote rachel bite Kaufer  she says the idea that there is this  informed engaged American population  that is watching these political events  and watching their elected leaders and  assessing their behavior and making a  judgement and it is just not true and as  she points out it's not really about  candidates swinging individual voters  from one candidate to another  it's about candidates engaging and  activating certain coalition's and  groups of voters now when other  political scientists are asked about  Rachel's theory many disagree and they  say no way there are swing voters  there's tons of people who voted for  Obama and then ultimately for Trump in  2016 or voted for Bernie in the primary  and then Trump or Hillary in the 2008  primary and what you write all these  different contingencies of people that  appear to swing from one side to the  other they exist of course anecdotally  and in small numbers but that really  what you are talking about is not a  number that swings elections what  changes in a more significant way is who  is engaged and t